From 79655942e8cfbf52fe2c3d7c560b15f7dc34c6fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Javier Maestro <jjmaestro@ieee.org>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 22:28:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] feat: key the extracted directory on the archive and
 its patches

`:dir` was the extracted directory itself, and Bazel does not hash the
contents of a directory input, as it says of every one:

    WARNING: input '' of <TARGET> is a directory;
    dependency checking of directories is unsound

An action reading one is not re-run when the tree changes, and this
rule re-downloading and re-patching does not help, because nothing
downstream can observe that it did. The failure mode is a patch that is
silently ignored: adding one to a version already built changes the
extracted tree and nothing else, so the build goes on compiling the
tree it staged first.

So `:dir` is now that directory copied into a tree artifact, with the
files that decide what it holds declared beside it: the repo
`lock.json`, whose `url` and `integrity` are the archive it came from
and which therefore covers a version re-pinned in place, and the
patches then applied to it, taken by content rather than by label since
editing a patch is the ordinary way to work on one. A change to either
re-keys the copy.

It is declared in the `//<VERSION>` package, not beside the extracted
directory in `//<VERSION>/<SOURCE>`. A tree artifact declared there sits
under the extracted directory's own path, and a consumer that takes both,
the tree for its contents and the directory as a `lib_source`, cannot
stage them: the parent wins and Bazel drops the tree as "obscured by"
it. One level up they are siblings and neither hides the other, and the
version package is where consumers ask for `:dir` anyway.

The directory as Bazel extracted it stays reachable as `:dir_path`, for
the uses that want the path and not the contents, such as computing a
prefix to strip from a compiler's `__FILE__`. Its comment now says
what the unsoundness costs rather than shrugging at it.

None of which makes a directory dependency sound, and `//lib:dir.bzl`
says as much: an edit reaching the extracted tree by some other route
still goes unnoticed. It closes the routes this rule itself takes.

The rule declares the tree as its runfiles as well as its files, so a
consumer staging it, a test, gets the tree rather than the extracted
directory Bazel would otherwise collect from the rule's own attributes.

The copy is the `copy_directory` toolchain binary with `--hardlink`,
the tree being copied again only when the repo has re-extracted it
wholesale.
